#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540334 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2 t + 2,\\,y=t,\\,t<2,\n$$\n\ndetermine the range of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ evaluate the endpoint: $x(2)=6$. The coefficient of $t$ is $2$, so the coordinate is monotone on the given half-line. The parameter endpoint is excluded. The range is $x<6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540342 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2 t + 5,\\,y=t,\\,t\\ge 3,\n$$\n\ndetermine the range of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ evaluate the endpoint: $x(3)=11$. The coefficient of $t$ is $2$, so the coordinate is monotone on the given half-line. The parameter endpoint is included. The range is $x\\ge 11.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540344 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=- 2 t^{2} + 6 t - 2,\\,y=t^{2} + 2 t + 1,\\,t>1,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ complete the square:\n\n$$\nx=- 2 t^{2} + 6 t - 2=\\frac{5}{2} - 2 \\left(t - \\frac{3}{2}\\right)^{2} .\n$$\n\nThe vertex parameter is $t=\\frac{3}{2}$, which is admissible. The vertex value is $\\frac{5}{2}$ and is attained. The coordinate is unbounded below. Consequently, $x\\le \\frac{5}{2}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ complete the square:\n\n$$\ny=t^{2} + 2 t + 1=\\left(t + 1\\right)^{2} .\n$$\n\nThe vertex parameter is $t=-1$, which is outside the given domain. On the given half-line the endpoint value is $4$, and the endpoint is excluded. The coordinate is unbounded above. Consequently, $y>4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540364 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2 t^{2} + 2 t,\\,y=t^{2} + 4 t - 5,\\,t\\le -2,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ complete the square:\n\n$$\nx=2 t^{2} + 2 t=2 \\left(t + \\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{2} - \\frac{1}{2} .\n$$\n\nThe vertex parameter is $t=- \\frac{1}{2}$, which is outside the given domain. On the given half-line the endpoint value is $4$, and the endpoint is included. The coordinate is unbounded above. Consequently, $x\\ge 4.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ complete the square:\n\n$$\ny=t^{2} + 4 t - 5=\\left(t + 2\\right)^{2} - 9 .\n$$\n\nThe vertex parameter is $t=-2$, which is admissible. The vertex value is $-9$ and is attained. The coordinate is unbounded above. Consequently, $y\\ge -9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540366 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=- t^{2} - 2 t - 2,\\,y=t^{2} - 2 t + 2,\\,t\\ge 3,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ complete the square:\n\n$$\nx=- t^{2} - 2 t - 2=- \\left(t + 1\\right)^{2} - 1 .\n$$\n\nThe vertex parameter is $t=-1$, which is outside the given domain. On the given half-line the endpoint value is $-17$, and the endpoint is included. The coordinate is unbounded below. Consequently, $x\\le -17.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ complete the square:\n\n$$\ny=t^{2} - 2 t + 2=\\left(t - 1\\right)^{2} + 1 .\n$$\n\nThe vertex parameter is $t=1$, which is outside the given domain. On the given half-line the endpoint value is $5$, and the endpoint is included. The coordinate is unbounded above. Consequently, $y\\ge 5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540388 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=- 2 t - 2,\\,y=t,\\,t>2,\n$$\n\ndetermine the range of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ evaluate the endpoint: $x(2)=-6$. The coefficient of $t$ is $-2$, so the coordinate is monotone on the given half-line. The parameter endpoint is excluded. The range is $x<-6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540454 :question/problem "Let $u=6i+8j+5k$ and $v=-i-4j-6k.$ Find $u\\times v.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 6 & 8 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -6\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -6\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((8)(-6)-(5)(-4))i-((6)(-6)-(5)(-1))j+((6)(-4)-(8)(-1))k \\\\  & =(-48-(-20))i-(-36-(-5))j+(-24-(-8))k \\\\  & =-28i+31j-16k \\\\  & =⟨-28,31,-16⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540456 :question/problem "Let $u=⟨3,\\frac{7}{2},-2⟩$ and $v=⟨\\frac{1}{2},-1,5⟩.$ Find $u\\times v.$\n\n$u\\times v=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & \\frac{7}{2} & -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{7}{2} & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 5\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)(5)-(-2)(-1))i-((3)(5)-(-2)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right))j+((3)(-1)-\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right))k \\\\  & =(\\frac{35}{2}-(2))i-(15-(-1))j+(-3-\\left(\\frac{7}{4}\\right))k \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{2}i-16j-\\frac{19}{4}k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{31}{2},-16,-\\frac{19}{4}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540471 :question/problem "Let $u=⟨\\frac{3}{5},2,5⟩$ and $v=⟨2,1,\\frac{7}{2}⟩.$ Find $u\\times v.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & 2 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 1 & \\frac{7}{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{7}{2}\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & 5 \\\\ 2 & \\frac{7}{2}\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((2)\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)-(5)(1))i-(\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)-(5)(2))j+(\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)(1)-(2)(2))k \\\\  & =(7-(5))i-(\\frac{21}{10}-(10))j+(\\frac{3}{5}-(4))k \\\\  & =2i+\\frac{79}{10}j-\\frac{17}{5}k \\\\  & =⟨2,\\frac{79}{10},-\\frac{17}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540476 :question/problem "Let $u=8i-j-4k$ and $v=4i-4j+4k.$ Find $u\\times v.$\n\n$u\\times v=$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\,i\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}$\\,j\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}$\\,k$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 8 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}8 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -4\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)(4)-(-4)(-4))i-((8)(4)-(-4)(4))j+((8)(-4)-(-1)(4))k \\\\  & =(-4-(16))i-(32-(-16))j+(-32-(-4))k \\\\  & =-20i-48j-28k \\\\  & =⟨-20,-48,-28⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540482 :question/problem "Let $u=4i+2j-\\frac{4}{3}k$ and $v=-4i+4j+\\frac{1}{3}k.$ Find $u\\times v.$\n\n$u\\times v=$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\,i\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}$\\,j\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}$\\,k$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & 2 & -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ -4 & 4 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 4 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ -4 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((2)\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)(4))i-((4)\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)(-4))j+((4)(4)-(2)(-4))k \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{16}{3}\\right))i-(\\frac{4}{3}-\\left(\\frac{16}{3}\\right))j+(16-(-8))k \\\\  & =6i+4j+24k \\\\  & =⟨6,4,24⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540483 :question/problem "Let $u=⟨-7,8,8⟩$ and $v=⟨6,-5,-7⟩.$ Find $u\\times v.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -7 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 6 & -5 & -7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 8 \\\\ -5 & -7\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 8 \\\\ 6 & -7\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 8 \\\\ 6 & -5\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((8)(-7)-(8)(-5))i-((-7)(-7)-(8)(6))j+((-7)(-5)-(8)(6))k \\\\  & =(-56-(-40))i-(49-(48))j+(35-(48))k \\\\  & =-16i-j-13k \\\\  & =⟨-16,-1,-13⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540489 :question/problem "Let $u=⟨-1,-1,-2⟩$ and $v=⟨-\\frac{5}{2},1,\\frac{5}{4}⟩.$ Find $u\\times v.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & 1 & \\frac{5}{4}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{5}{4}\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{5}{4}\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)-(-2)(1))i-((-1)\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)-(-2)\\left(-\\frac{5}{2}\\right))j+((-1)(1)-(-1)\\left(-\\frac{5}{2}\\right))k \\\\  & =(-\\frac{5}{4}-(-2))i-(-\\frac{5}{4}-(5))j+(-1-\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right))k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}i+\\frac{25}{4}j-\\frac{7}{2}k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{25}{4},-\\frac{7}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540496 :question/problem "Let $u=⟨4,3,2⟩$ and $v=⟨6,1,7⟩.$ Find $u\\times v.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((3)(7)-(2)(1))i-((4)(7)-(2)(6))j+((4)(1)-(3)(6))k \\\\  & =(21-(2))i-(28-(12))j+(4-(18))k \\\\  & =19i-16j-14k \\\\  & =⟨19,-16,-14⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540497 :question/problem "Let $u=⟨-3,2,1⟩$ and $v=⟨4,-4,8⟩.$ Find $u\\times v.$\n\n$u\\times v=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -4\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((2)(8)-(1)(-4))i-((-3)(8)-(1)(4))j+((-3)(-4)-(2)(4))k \\\\  & =(16-(-4))i-(-24-(4))j+(12-(8))k \\\\  & =20i+28j+4k \\\\  & =⟨20,28,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540499 :question/problem "Let $u=-\\frac{6}{5}i+2j+k$ and $v=-\\frac{7}{2}i-4j+6k.$ Find $u\\times v.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -\\frac{6}{5} & 2 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & -4 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{6}{5} & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & 6\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{6}{5} & 2 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & -4\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((2)(6)-(1)(-4))i-(\\left(-\\frac{6}{5}\\right)(6)-(1)\\left(-\\frac{7}{2}\\right))j+(\\left(-\\frac{6}{5}\\right)(-4)-(2)\\left(-\\frac{7}{2}\\right))k \\\\  & =(12-(-4))i-(-\\frac{36}{5}-\\left(-\\frac{7}{2}\\right))j+(\\frac{24}{5}-(-7))k \\\\  & =16i+\\frac{37}{10}j+\\frac{59}{5}k \\\\  & =⟨16,\\frac{37}{10},\\frac{59}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541055 :question/problem "![](images/7c/7c5e30b2851a8268c5193c2f87e3c6ab77645db4d165d83150fb504ba06ef5c2.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $5,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $H(6,0).$\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=6$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541058 :question/problem "The eagle's path traces a curve $\\mathcal{C}$ in the $xy$-plane. Suppose that the eagle is at the point $P$ on $\\mathcal{C}$ at time $t.$ Expressed in terms of $t,$ what function gives the slope of the tangent to $\\mathcal{C}$ at $P?$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to $\\mathcal{C}$ at time $t$ equals$\\,$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope of the tangent to $\\mathcal{C}$ equals $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$ To calculate this, we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nIn parts 1 and 2, we found\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{t}^{2}-2}{\\left(\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{5}({t}^{2}-2)({t}^{2}+25).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541060 :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4 \\\\ \\frac{x+4}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2 \\\\ \\frac{y-2}{3\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541062 :question/problem "![](images/13/13036c467cbe4fdd245b12c481de08ac1eb14bbfcc50653309a913ab1e9d8463.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(4,-2),$ $a=7$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541063 :question/problem "The eagle's total displacement in the $y$-direction over the first $6$ seconds is given by $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{6}{y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$ Calculate this displacement.\n\nThe eagle's total displacement in the $y$-direction over the first $6$ seconds is$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "In part 7, we found that the antiderivative of ${y}^{′}(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-2t+C.\n$$\n\nBy the fundamental theorem of calculus, we take the antiderivative and evaluate the difference at the bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =y(t){|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-2t)|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}(6)^{3}-2(6))-(\\frac{1}{3}(0)^{3}-2(0)) \\\\  & =60-0 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eagle's total displacement in the $y$-direction over the first $6$ seconds is $60\\,\\text{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541073 :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}={x}^{7/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{7}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{7/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/5+1}}{(\\frac{7}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{12/5}}{\\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{12}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{12/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{12}\\cdot \\frac{12}{5}{x}^{12/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{7/5}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541078 :question/problem "The tangent line to the eagle's flight path is horizontal at $t=${{field-1}}$\\,\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nIn parts 1 and 2, we found\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}-2=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $-\\sqrt{2}\\notin (0,\\infty ),$ so we only consider the value $t=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\sqrt{2})=\\frac{5}{27}\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the curve's tangent is horizontal when $t=\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541081 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨y\\operatorname{cos}y+y{x}^{3},\\hspace{0.222em}\\sqrt[3]{x}+y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square with vertices $(-1,0),(1,0),(1,2),$ and $(-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/fa/fa98f3db31860a463d0a551d5297895e61a6c041d852c04de04c44a194510961.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y{x}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{x}+y.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}y+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3{x}^{2}y+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}y+x)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{-1}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[((1)^{3}y+1)-((-1)^{3}y+(-1))]\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2y+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =[{y}^{2}+2y]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =((2)^{2}+2(2))-0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541086 :question/problem "![](images/a4/a41c728f9a82b563203fa0eb6944617948cf12c048be2553b803b06820d0b896.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(-3,4)$ with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $6,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(-1,4).$\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-3,4),$ $a=2$ and $b=6.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541087 :question/problem "![](images/cc/cc9e723494b5d592b97ff1b4795eb1bc0ca7be0c4608591792eb2ac5515a9811.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=1$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/6e/6eeff954ea6f159a3bdc781361da094319bcf06a5365d12e894228bb172a16e6.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541115 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{4/5}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{4}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{4/5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{4/5+1}}{(\\frac{4}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{9/5}}{\\left(\\frac{9}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{9}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{9/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{9}\\cdot \\frac{9}{5}{x}^{9/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{4/5}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541118 :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(5\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{50} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{3\\sqrt{7}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{63} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{50}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{63}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{50}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{63}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541125 :question/problem "![](images/bb/bbfc3c75c4dfeb96b6c17e8b3991238bfedde590297bbb551a0386b0a9508d00.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $10$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $I(10,0).$\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=10$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541138 :question/problem "![](images/ba/baa8ce8fbfb4ed3acb3a9de52901b80e6acf81613e0c98ed04082fec3f653c0f.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541140 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}={x}^{-5}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-5$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-5+1}}{-5+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4}}{-4}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{4}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541141 :question/problem "![](images/ea/ea74c15f3d02478a7486f967b7d2eebc780d1b5bd53ac21a81934b6438531f6d.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $5,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541144 :question/problem "![](images/4b/4bccea09ccf18444f5b9505a8a753a9065ea97a5dc622208c281926c17cd5de1.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-2,3),$ $a=3$ and $b=6.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541145 :question/problem "![](images/00/008a79d8328b5b58cdc68a39a921125a1efa7e0b46a663b1ede9dc6773fb48e8.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=1.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/82/82436a7f248ed63683a3164d8c372433abb6f919567defa05700ebf88c50308f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541147 :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{7} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541152 :question/problem "![](images/41/410d6ec438e066487cd6de9d4bcbf429362a5a86d5893e7820a832d2fffb9bbd.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/06/06e4a1a4d50943deff0b50eb510ca7ad4676ad6015a9503f7ce279f249078858.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541232 :question/problem "Find $(u(t)f(t))^{′}$ where $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 3 e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=t^{2} + t + 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)f(t))^{′}=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find $u^{′}(t)$ and $f^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 e^{t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{t} \\\\ - 3 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\u^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2} + t + 1)=2 t + 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)f(t))^{′}&=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t)\\\\&=(2 t + 1)\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 3 e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+(t^{2} + t + 1)\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{t} \\\\ - 3 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\left(2 t + 1\\right) \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 3 \\left(2 t + 1\\right) e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{2 t + 1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2 \\left(t^{2} + t + 1\\right)}{t} \\\\ - 3 \\left(t^{2} + t + 1\\right) e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{t^{2} + t + 1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}4 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 t + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 + \\frac{2}{t} \\\\ 3 \\left(- t^{2} - 3 t - 2\\right) e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541242 :question/problem "Find $(u(t)f(t))^{′}$ where $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 2 e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=t^{2} + 2 t - 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)f(t))^{′}=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find $u^{′}(t)$ and $f^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 2 e^{t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - 2 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\u^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2} + 2 t - 2)=2 t + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)f(t))^{′}&=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t)\\\\&=(2 t + 2)\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 2 e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+(t^{2} + 2 t - 2)\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - 2 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\left(2 t + 2\\right) \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 2 \\left(2 t + 2\\right) e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{2 t + 2}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3 \\left(t^{2} + 2 t - 2\\right)}{t} \\\\ - 2 \\left(t^{2} + 2 t - 2\\right) e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{t^{2} + 2 t - 2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t - 6 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 + \\frac{6}{t} \\\\ 2 t \\left(- t - 4\\right) e^{t} \\\\ 1 + \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541248 :question/problem "Find $(u(t)f(t))^{′}$ where $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 3 e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=t^{2} + t + 3.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)f(t))^{′}=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find $u^{′}(t)$ and $f^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 e^{t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{1}{t} \\\\ - 3 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\u^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2} + t + 3)=2 t + 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)f(t))^{′}&=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t)\\\\&=(2 t + 1)\\begin{bmatrix}- \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 3 e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+(t^{2} + t + 3)\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{1}{t} \\\\ - 3 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\left(2 t + 1\\right) \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 3 \\left(2 t + 1\\right) e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{2 t + 1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{t^{2} + t + 3}{t} \\\\ - 3 \\left(t^{2} + t + 3\\right) e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{t^{2} + t + 3}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - t - \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 1 - \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ 3 \\left(- t^{2} - 3 t - 4\\right) e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{3}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541260 :question/problem "Find $(u(t)f(t))^{′}$ where $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=t^{2} + t + 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)f(t))^{′}=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find $u^{′}(t)$ and $f^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- e^{t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\u^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2} + t + 1)=2 t + 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)f(t))^{′}&=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t)\\\\&=(2 t + 1)\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+(t^{2} + t + 1)\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\left(2 t + 1\\right) \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - \\left(2 t + 1\\right) e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{2 t + 1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3 \\left(t^{2} + t + 1\\right)}{t} \\\\ - \\left(t^{2} + t + 1\\right) e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{t^{2} + t + 1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t - 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 - \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ \\left(- t^{2} - 3 t - 2\\right) e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
